<!-- SECTION 1: Introduction -->
<section id="csc-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is a Cosecant Ratio Calculator?</h2>
  <p>A cosecant ratio calculator finds the cosecant of an acute angle in a right triangle from two side lengths: the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> and the opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />. It divides the first by the second, giving <span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=c/a]]')} />. Cosecant is the reciprocal of sine: wherever sine gives opposite over hypotenuse, cosecant gives hypotenuse over opposite, so the two values always multiply to 1.</p>
  <p>The calculator needs no angle input and no adjacent side, because the ratio <span set:html={rich('[[c/a]]')} /> is fully determined by those two lengths. The answer is a pure number with no units, since centimetres divided by centimetres cancel out, and it describes the hypotenuse as a multiple of the opposite side. For a 5 cm hypotenuse and a 3 cm opposite side, <span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=5/3\\approx1.6667]]')} />, meaning the hypotenuse is about 1.67 times the opposite side.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 2: Understanding the right triangle -->
<section id="csc-triangle" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Hypotenuse and Opposite Side</h2>

  <h3>What Is a Right Triangle?</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has one angle of exactly <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} />, the angle between two perpendicular lines, like a wall meeting a level floor. The hypotenuse sits opposite that right angle and is always the longest side. Because the three angles must total <span set:html={rich('[[180^\\circ]]')} />, the remaining two angles share exactly 90 degrees and are therefore both acute. Cosecant belongs to this geometry because its formula compares two of those sides, the hypotenuse and the opposite side, in a fixed relationship.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Opposite Side?</h3>
  <p>The opposite side is the side directly across from the reference angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />: it is the one side of the triangle that does not touch the vertex of <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />. Which side counts as opposite depends on the reference angle, so switching from angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> to angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> swaps the labels of the two legs. In practical problems the opposite side often represents a vertical height, an elevation difference, or the perpendicular leg of a structure. Getting the reference angle right matters, because the wrong angle silently swaps the opposite and adjacent sides.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Hypotenuse?</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse is the side opposite the right angle, and two facts identify it in any diagram, however the triangle is rotated: it faces the small square marking the <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} /> corner, and it is the longest side. In a valid, non-degenerate triangle it is strictly longer than either leg. In real settings it may represent a ladder leaning on a wall, a sloping distance, a line of sight, a roof rafter, or a diagonal support.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Adjacent Side?</h3>
  <p>The adjacent side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is the leg that touches angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> without being the hypotenuse. It plays no role in the cosecant formula, which is why the calculator never asks for it. Side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> becomes relevant for cosine, tangent, secant, and cotangent, so keep it in mind when a different ratio is the goal.</p>

