</section>

<!-- SECTION 3: What is cosecant -->
<section id="csc-whatis" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding Cosecant in Trigonometry</h2>
  <p>Cosecant compares the complete sloping side of a right triangle with the side opposite the reference angle:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\csc(A)=\\frac{c}{a}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)]]')} /> is the cosecant of angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse, and <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is the opposite side. The ratio answers one question: how many opposite-side lengths fit into the hypotenuse?</p>

  <h3>Cosecant as the Reciprocal of Sine</h3>
  <p>Start with the sine formula:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{a}{c}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Taking the reciprocal reverses the fraction, moving the hypotenuse into the numerator and the opposite side into the denominator:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\frac{1}{\\sin(A)}=\\frac{c}{a}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Since cosecant is defined as the reciprocal of sine:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\csc(A)=\\frac{1}{\\sin(A)}=\\frac{c}{a}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>A concrete check makes the flip visible. With <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=3/5=0.6]]')} />, the reciprocal is <span set:html={rich('[[1/0.6=5/3\\approx1.6667]]')} />, which is exactly the cosecant. Both numbers describe the same triangle from reciprocal viewpoints, and multiplying them always returns 1.</p>

  <h3>Why Cosecant Is Greater Than 1</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse is longer than the opposite side, so <span set:html={rich('[[c \\gt a]]')} /> and therefore <span set:html={rich('[[c/a \\gt 1]]')} />. A quick numeric check: with <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[a=8]]')} />, the ratio is <span set:html={rich('[[10/8=1.25]]')} />, above 1 as required. For a large acute angle the opposite side is nearly as long as the hypotenuse, so the ratio sits just above 1, but it can never equal 1 in a valid, non-degenerate right triangle.</p>

  <h3>What Does a Cosecant Value Mean?</h3>
  <p>A cosecant of 2 means the hypotenuse is exactly twice the opposite side; 1.6667 means about 1.6667 times. Read the number as a multiplier, not an angle: it carries no degrees and no metres or feet, because the two side units cancel in the division.</p>

  <h3>Why Is Cosecant Different From Cosine?</h3>
  <p>The names look alike, but the relationships differ:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\cos(A)=\\frac{b}{c}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\csc(A)=\\frac{c}{a}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Cosine compares the adjacent side with the hypotenuse, while cosecant compares the hypotenuse with the opposite side. The pairing to remember: cosecant is the reciprocal of sine, whereas secant is the reciprocal of cosine. Mixing up the two co-functions is one of the most common errors in trigonometry.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('csc-reciprocal.svg')} alt="Three stages: 3-4-5 triangle, sine 3/5=0.6, cosecant 5/3=1.6667, identity csc(A)=1/sin(A)" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Sine and cosecant are reciprocals: flipping 3/5 gives 5/3, and csc(A) = 1/sin(A).</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 4: Formula derivation -->
<section id="csc-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>The Cosecant Formula Explained Step by Step</h2>
  <p>The formula is not arbitrary; it follows directly from the definition of sine. Start with the sine ratio:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{a}{c}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Take the reciprocal of both sides. The numerator and denominator change places:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\frac{1}{\\sin(A)}=\\frac{c}{a}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Because cosecant is the reciprocal of sine by definition:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\csc(A)=\\frac{c}{a}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The hypotenuse appears in the numerator because it was the denominator of sine; the opposite side drops to the denominator because it was the numerator of sine. That is the whole derivation.</p>

  <h3>Fully Worked Example: A 3-4-5 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ cm}]]')} />:</p>
  <p>Step 1: Write the formula. <span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=c/a]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 2: Substitute the measurements. <span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=5/3]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 3: Divide. <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\csc(A)\\approx1.6667}]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 4: Verify using sine. <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=3/5=0.6]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[1/0.6\\approx1.6667]]')} />. Both routes land on the same value because they are reciprocal views of one triangle. Note that 1.6667 is the rounded decimal; <span set:html={rich('[[5/3]]')} /> is the exact ratio for these side lengths.</p>
</section>

