<!-- SECTION 7: Twelve practical examples -->
<section id="csc-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Real-Life Examples of Using the Cosecant Ratio</h2>
  <p>Cosecant is a mathematical ratio rather than a number reported on its own at a work site. Its value lies in describing the hypotenuse-to-opposite-side relationship, verifying calculations, and feeding formulas that call for cosecant. Each example below assumes a simplified right triangle.</p>

  <h3>Example 5: Understanding a Ladder's Length Relative to Its Height</h3>
  <p>A 5.5 m ladder reaches 4.5 m up a vertical wall. The ladder is the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c=5.5\\text{ m}]]')} />; the reach is the opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a=4.5\\text{ m}]]')} />.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=5.5/4.5]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\csc(A)\\approx1.2222}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>The ladder is about 1.2222 times the vertical height, and <span set:html={rich('[[c=a\\times\\csc(A)]]')} /> recovers the ladder length from the height. This ratio describes geometry only, not ladder safety.</p>

  <h3>Example 6: Calculating a Roof Rafter's Cosecant Ratio</h3>
  <p>A 6.5 m roof rafter has a vertical rise of 4 m: the rafter is the sloping hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c=6.5\\text{ m}]]')} />; the rise is the opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a=4\\text{ m}]]')} />.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=6.5/4]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\csc(A)=1.625}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>The rafter is 1.625 times the vertical rise. This calculation does not verify structural suitability or roof-design compliance.</p>

  <h3>Example 7: Evaluating a Ramp's Cosecant Ratio</h3>
  <p>A ramp has a 9.5 m sloping length and a 0.75 m vertical rise: <span set:html={rich('[[c=9.5\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[a=0.75\\text{ m}]]')} />.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=9.5/0.75]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\csc(A)\\approx12.6667}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>The ratio is large because the sloping length dwarfs the vertical rise, as a gentle ramp should. The ratio alone does not determine accessibility or building-code compliance.</p>

  <h3>Example 8: A Surveying Line With a Known Elevation Difference</h3>
  <p>A simplified surveying measurement records a 50 m straight-line distance and a 30 m vertical difference: the straight line is the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c=50\\text{ m}]]')} />; with the vertical difference as the opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a=30\\text{ m}]]')} />.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=50/30]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\csc(A)\\approx1.6667}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>The sloping distance is about 1.6667 times the vertical component. Real surveying can require additional corrections beyond this simplified model.</p>

  <h3>Example 9: Calculating a Guy Wire's Ratio</h3>
  <p>An 18 m guy wire has a 10 m vertical separation between its endpoints: the wire is the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c=18\\text{ m}]]')} />; the separation is the opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a=10\\text{ m}]]')} />.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=18/10]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\csc(A)=1.8}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>The wire is 1.8 times the vertical separation. The ratio does not determine wire tension, anchor strength, or structural safety.</p>

  <h3>Example 10: Evaluating a Staircase Stringer's Ratio</h3>
  <p>A simplified 5 m staircase stringer has a 2.8 m vertical rise: the stringer is the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ m}]]')} />; the rise is the opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a=2.8\\text{ m}]]')} />.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=5/2.8]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\csc(A)\\approx1.7857}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>The diagonal is about 1.7857 times the vertical rise: a simplified geometric calculation, not a complete staircase-design assessment.</p>

  <h3>Example 11: An Aircraft's Straight Flight Segment</h3>
  <p>An aircraft travels 5 km along a straight path while gaining 1.5 km in altitude: the path is the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ km}]]')} />; the altitude gain is the opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a=1.5\\text{ km}]]')} />.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=5/1.5]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\csc(A)\\approx3.3333}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>The flight distance far exceeds the vertical gain, hence the large ratio. This is an idealized example, not a complete analysis of a changing flight path.</p>

  <h3>Example 12: A Mechanical Brace</h3>
  <p>A diagonal brace is 1,700 mm long with an 800 mm vertical component: the brace is the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c=1700\\text{ mm}]]')} />; the vertical component is the opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a=800\\text{ mm}]]')} />.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=1700/800]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\csc(A)=2.125}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Millimetres work as well as metres when both sides share the unit. The ratio does not establish the brace's strength.</p>

  <h3>Example 13: A Crane Boom</h3>
  <p>A simplified 10 m crane boom has an 8 m vertical component: the boom is the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c=10\\text{ m}]]')} />; the vertical component is the opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a=8\\text{ m}]]')} />.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=10/8]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\csc(A)=1.25}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>The boom's length is only 1.25 times its vertical component. The ratio does not establish lifting capacity or operating safety.</p>

  <h3>Example 14: A Line of Sight to the Top of a Tree</h3>
  <p>A 20 m line of sight to a tree's top has a 12 m vertical difference from the observer's eye level: the sight line is the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c=20\\text{ m}]]')} />; the vertical difference is the opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a=12\\text{ m}]]')} />.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=20/12]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\csc(A)\\approx1.6667}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>The sight distance is about 1.6667 times the vertical difference. The result does not give the tree's total height above the ground.</p>

  <h3>Example 15: A Hiking Route's Straight Incline</h3>
  <p>A straight hiking segment slopes 100 m with an 80 m elevation gain: the slope is the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c=100\\text{ m}]]')} />; the gain is the opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a=80\\text{ m}]]')} />.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=100/80]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\csc(A)=1.25}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>The ratio sits close to 1 because the vertical component is a large share of the slope. Actual hiking routes can have varying gradients.</p>

  <h3>Example 16: An Awning Support</h3>
  <p>A 3.5 m awning support has a 1.2 m vertical component: the support is the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c=3.5\\text{ m}]]')} />; the vertical component is the opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a=1.2\\text{ m}]]')} />.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=3.5/1.2]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\csc(A)\\approx2.9167}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>The support is about 2.9167 times its vertical component. The result does not establish structural suitability.</p>

