<!-- SECTION 8: Cosecant and angle size -->
<section id="csc-angle" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Relationship Between Cosecant and Angle Size</h2>
  <p>Cosecant moves in the opposite direction from the angle. When the acute angle is small, the opposite side is short relative to the hypotenuse, so <span set:html={rich('[[c/a]]')} /> is large. As the angle grows, the opposite side lengthens toward the hypotenuse, and the ratio shrinks. The verified reference values below show the pattern:</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/30">
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Angle A</th>
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Sine sin(A)</th>
          <th class="py-3 font-semibold text-on-surface">Cosecant csc(A)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="text-on-surface-variant">
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">30 deg</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">0.5</td><td class="py-2.5">2</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">45 deg</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">0.7071</td><td class="py-2.5">1.4142</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">60 deg</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">0.8660</td><td class="py-2.5">1.1547</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">90 deg</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">1</td><td class="py-2.5">1, as a limiting value</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>As the angle approaches <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} />, the opposite side approaches the hypotenuse and the ratio approaches 1, while staying above 1 for every valid, non-degenerate right triangle. The 90-degree row is a limiting reference only, since angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> must be acute. As the angle approaches <span set:html={rich('[[0^\\circ]]')} />, the opposite side shrinks toward zero and the ratio grows without bound. A ratio near 1 signals a relatively large acute angle; a large ratio signals a relatively small one.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('csc-angle-change.svg')} alt="Three triangles at 30, 45, 60 degrees with cosecant values 2, 1.4142, 1.1547; ratio falls as angle grows" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">As the opposite side becomes a larger share of the hypotenuse, csc(A) moves closer to 1.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>The calculator does not accept an angle input; it computes the ratio from the two side lengths. These reference values serve as orientation and as check values when verifying a result.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 9: Recover a side using cosecant -->
<section id="csc-recover" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Using Cosecant to Find the Hypotenuse</h2>
  <p>The formula rearranges cleanly. Start with:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\csc(A)=\\frac{c}{a}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Multiply both sides by <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{c=a\\times\\csc(A)}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The hypotenuse is the opposite side multiplied by the cosecant ratio. With <span set:html={rich('[[a=6\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=2.5]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[c=6\\times2.5]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{c=15\\text{ m}}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The result is longer than the opposite side, as every hypotenuse must be.</p>
  <h3>How to Recover the Opposite Side</h3>
  <p>Solving for <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> instead gives:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{a=\\frac{c}{\\csc(A)}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>With <span set:html={rich('[[c=12\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=2]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[a=12/2]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{a=6\\text{ m}}]]')} /></p>
  <p>These are mathematical rearrangements of the same ratio. The calculator on this page computes the ratio itself; it does not provide separate hypotenuse-finding or opposite-side-finding modes.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 10: Common mistakes -->
<section id="csc-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating Cosecant</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Confusing cosecant with sine.</strong> Sine is <span set:html={rich('[[a/c]]')} />; cosecant is <span set:html={rich('[[c/a]]')} />. What goes wrong: reporting 0.6 for a 3-4-5 triangle. Fix: put the hypotenuse on top.</li>
    <li><strong>Reversing the formula to <span set:html={rich('[[a/c]]')} /> instead of <span set:html={rich('[[c/a]]')} />.</strong> What goes wrong: the answer is the sine, always below 1. Fix: check that the longer side is the numerator.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing cosecant with secant.</strong> Secant is <span set:html={rich('[[c/b]]')} />, the reciprocal of cosine. What goes wrong: using the adjacent side. Fix: cosecant pairs with sine and the opposite side.</li>
    <li><strong>Using the adjacent side instead of the opposite side.</strong> What goes wrong: computing <span set:html={rich('[[c/b]]')} /> by mistake. Fix: confirm the side across from angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> before dividing.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming cosecant is measured in degrees.</strong> What goes wrong: writing 1.6667 degrees. Fix: the ratio is a pure number, not an angle.</li>
    <li><strong>Reporting a cosecant ratio with metres or centimetres.</strong> What goes wrong: the units were already cancelled. Fix: drop the unit from the answer.</li>
    <li><strong>Accepting a result less than 1 for a valid triangle.</strong> What goes wrong: trusting a swapped-input calculation. Fix: a valid result always exceeds 1.</li>
    <li><strong>Entering a hypotenuse shorter than the opposite side.</strong> What goes wrong: the inputs describe an impossible triangle. Fix: the hypotenuse field must hold the longer measurement.</li>
    <li><strong>Entering zero or negative side lengths.</strong> What goes wrong: division by zero or a meaningless negative ratio. Fix: use positive lengths only.</li>
    <li><strong>Mixing measurement units.</strong> What goes wrong: centimetres divided by metres skew the ratio by a factor of 100. Fix: convert both sides to one unit first.</li>
    <li><strong>Rounding too early.</strong> What goes wrong: rounding <span set:html={rich('[[5/3]]')} /> to 1.67 before multiplying back adds avoidable error. Fix: keep the fraction or extra digits until the final step.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing the reference angle with the other acute angle.</strong> What goes wrong: the opposite and adjacent labels swap. Fix: mark angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> on the diagram first.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming a large cosecant ratio is an angle.</strong> What goes wrong: reading 12.6667 as degrees. Fix: it is a multiplier, so the hypotenuse is 12.6667 times the opposite side.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming cosecant alone determines the hypotenuse.</strong> What goes wrong: expecting one number to produce a length. Fix: the ratio needs the opposite side too, via <span set:html={rich('[[c=a\\times\\csc(A)]]')} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing the reciprocal of sine with the reciprocal of cosine.</strong> What goes wrong: using secant where cosecant belongs. Fix: sine pairs with cosecant; cosine pairs with secant.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Wrong Versus Correct</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />:</p>
  <p>Incorrect: <span set:html={rich('[[3/5=0.6]]')} />. This is the sine ratio, not the cosecant ratio.</p>
  <p>Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=5/3\\approx1.6667]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Verify: <span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=1/\\sin(A)]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[1/0.6\\approx1.6667]]')} />.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 11: Verification -->
<section id="csc-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Your Cosecant Result Is Valid</h2>
  <p>A valid, non-degenerate right triangle requires:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c \\gt a \\gt 0]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Therefore <span set:html={rich('[[c/a \\gt 1]]')} />. Both measurements must use compatible units, and the calculated ratio must exceed 1. A result of exactly 1 is only a limiting value as the acute angle approaches 90 degrees, not a result for a genuine triangle. A ratio below 1 usually indicates reversed inputs, an incorrect side label, or incompatible measurements.</p>
  <h3>Method 1: Check the Sine Reciprocal</h3>
  <p>Compute <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=a/c]]')} />, then <span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=1/\\sin(A)]]')} />. For the 3-4-5 triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=3/5=0.6]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[1/0.6\\approx1.6667]]')} />, matching the direct division.</p>
  <h3>Method 2: Check the Pythagorean Theorem</h3>
  <p>If the adjacent side is known or needed, verify <span set:html={rich('[[b=\\sqrt{c^2-a^2}]]')} />. For <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[b=\\sqrt{25-9}=4]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[3^2+4^2=5^2]]')} />. This confirms the side lengths form a right triangle.</p>
  <h3>Method 3: Check the Ratio by Multiplication</h3>
  <p>Verify <span set:html={rich('[[a\\times\\csc(A)\\approx c]]')} />. For <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=5/3]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[3\\times\\frac{5}{3}=5]]')} />. Small discrepancies may appear when a displayed decimal was rounded; the exact fraction removes them.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 12: Choosing a ratio -->
<section id="csc-vs" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Cosecant vs. Sine, Secant, and Other Trigonometric Ratios</h2>
  <p>Each ratio compares a different pair of sides:</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/30">
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Ratio</th>
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Formula</th>
          <th class="py-3 font-semibold text-on-surface">What it compares</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="text-on-surface-variant">
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Sine</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">sin(A) = a/c</td><td class="py-2.5">Opposite side to hypotenuse</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Cosecant</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">csc(A) = c/a</td><td class="py-2.5">Hypotenuse to opposite side</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Cosine</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">cos(A) = b/c</td><td class="py-2.5">Adjacent side to hypotenuse</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Secant</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">sec(A) = c/b</td><td class="py-2.5">Hypotenuse to adjacent side</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Tangent</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">tan(A) = a/b</td><td class="py-2.5">Opposite side to adjacent side</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Cotangent</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">cot(A) = b/a</td><td class="py-2.5">Adjacent side to opposite side</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The reciprocal pairs are sine with cosecant and cosine with secant: <span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=1/\\sin(A)]]')} />, whereas <span set:html={rich('[[\\sec(A)=1/\\cos(A)]]')} />. Cosecant and secant are not interchangeable. Sine suits direct measurement problems with a known opposite side and hypotenuse; cosecant suits formulas built around the reciprocal and recovering the hypotenuse from a known cosecant value.</p>
  <p>For the neighbouring ratios, see the <a href="/sine-ratio-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Sine Ratio Calculator</a>, the <a href="/secant-ratio-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Secant Ratio Calculator</a>, the <a href="/cotangent-ratio-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Cotangent Ratio Calculator</a>, and the <a href="/right-triangle-trig-ratios-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Right Triangle Trig Ratios Calculator</a>. The <a href="/right-triangle-side-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Right Triangle Side Calculator</a> helps when a side length itself is the unknown.</p>
</section>

